        "problem": "The data below is taken from the table of values of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z)$ for the standard normal distribution. Given that $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ compute $P(Z<-0.93).$\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ | $\\,0.1762\\,$ | $\\,0.1736\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | $\\,0.2033\\,$ | $\\,0.2005\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ |",
        "worked_solution": "If $Z\u223cN(0,1),$ then by the definition of the cumulative distribution function $\u03a6(z),$ \n\n$$\nP(Z\\le z)=\u03a6(z).\n$$\n\nAlso, since $Z$ is continuous, \n\n$$\nP(Z<-0.93)=P(Z\\le -0.93)=\u03a6(-0.93).\n$$\n\nThe required probability is represented by the area shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14069404/assets/0d1e28f38a69fb9b75d67ef1753f23ce01b6328a5bbcd30c7dfb7fd5e24caec4.png)\n\nNow, we determine the value of $\u03a6(-0.93)$ from the table:\n\n- We first select the row that corresponds to $-0.9$ and the column that corresponds to $0.03.$\n\n- Then, we take the value that lies at the intersection:\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ | $\\,0.1762\\,$ | $\\,0.1736\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | $\\,0.2033\\,$ | $\\,0.2005\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.7\\,$ | $\\,0.2358\\,$ | $\\,0.2327\\,$ | $\\,0.2296\\,$ |\n\nTherefore, according to the table, we have\n\n$$\nP(Z\\le -0.93)=\u03a6(-0.93)=0.1762.\n$$",
